    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d374">

                <pb facs="0398" n="300" sameAs="frus:frus1930v02/pg300"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">761.93 Conference, 1930/26</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">of a Conversation
                        With the Chinese Vice Minister for Foreign Affairs</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Frank Lee</hi>)<anchor n="64" xml:id="d374-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1930-05-21">May 21, 1930</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>Vice Minister Lee informed me today that Moh Teh-hui, Chinese delegate to the
                    Sino-Russian Conference, had arrived in Moscow and it was expected that parleys
                    would soon begin. Mr. Lee stated that he felt that matters would not be settled
                    until the 1924 agreement<anchor n="65" xml:id="d374-f2"/> had been changed as
                    the plan therein provided for, for joint operation of the railway with each
                    nationality having an equal number of directors, had not worked as either side
                    could prevent effective operation of the Board of Directors by absence from
                    meetings, thus throwing the entire business of the railway into the hands of the
                    manager of the railway, a Russian. He stated that Moh Teh-hui had been well
                    received in Moscow, having been met at the station by Karakhan<anchor n="66"
                        xml:id="d374-f3"/> and was rapidly getting acquainted. He stated, however,
                    that he <pb facs="0399" n="301" xml:id="pg301"/> feared Karakhan would demand a
                    broadening of the powers of the Chinese delegate, Moh, to include commercial and
                    diplomatic relations and that the Chinese Government would have to refuse to do
                    this.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Nelson Trusler
                    Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d374-f1" n="64" type="source" xml:id="d374-f1-content">
                <p>Copy transmitted to the Department without covering despatch; received July 17,
                    1930.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d374-f2" n="65" xml:id="d374-f2-content">
                <p>For texts of agreements signed at Peking, May 31, 1924, see <ref
                        target="frus1924v01#pg_495"><hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>,
                        1924, vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">i</hi>, p. 495</ref>; for report on
                    agreement signed at Mukden, see telegram No. 377, October 4, from the Chargé in
                    China, <ref target="frus1924v01#pg_510"><hi rendition="#italic">ibid.</hi>, p.
                        510</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d374-f3" n="66" xml:id="d374-f3-content">
                <p>L. M. Karakhan, chief Soviet delegate and former Soviet Ambassador in China.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
